course: world
lesson: 120
title: Time zones: why it's night in Sydney when it's day in London
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: As the Earth spins, morning sweeps round the planet, so it cannot be the same clock time everywhere and still be light at noon. The world is cut into time zones, each with its own clock time, so that noon stays near the middle of the day wherever you are.
explain: The Earth turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, which is 15 degrees every hour, so time zones are roughly 15-degree slices. Borders make them wiggly, some places such as India use half-hours, and China uses one zone for the whole wide country, so in its far west the Sun can rise at 9 or 10 am by the clock.
explain: Places to the east are ahead (later in the day); places to the west are behind. In January, when Jake has breakfast at 9 am in London, his cousin in Sydney is brushing her teeth for bed at 8 pm, 11 hours ahead. New York is usually 5 hours behind London, and Kyiv is 2 hours ahead.
explain: For a flight, add the flying time on the clock of the place you left, then change to the clock of the place you land. London to New York, leaving 11:00 and flying 8 hours: 19:00 London time, which is 14:00 in New York.
explain: To avoid mix-ups, pilots, ships, scientists and games use one world time, UTC, almost the same as Greenwich Mean Time, and sensible people say "6 pm your time" or "6 pm my time".
fact: A time zone is an area that uses the same clock time.
fact: The Earth turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, which is 15 degrees every hour, so time zones are roughly 15 degrees wide and there are about 24 main ones.
fact: Places to the east are ahead of you in time, and places to the west are behind.
fact: In January Sydney is 11 hours ahead of London, so 9 am in London is 8 pm in Sydney.
fact: New York is usually 5 hours behind London, so 2 pm in London is 9 am in New York.
fact: Kyiv is 2 hours ahead of London, so 7 pm in Kyiv is 5 pm in London.
fact: Dubai is 4 hours ahead of Britain in winter, so 6 pm in Manchester is 10 pm in Dubai.
fact: India is 5 and a half hours ahead of London in winter, and China uses one time zone for the whole country.
fact: UTC, Coordinated Universal Time, is the world's standard time, almost the same as Greenwich Mean Time; pilots call it Zulu time.
fact: Italy in summer is 2 hours ahead of UTC, so 18:00 UTC is 20:00 in Italy.
fact: Hawaii is almost a whole day behind New Zealand, because the International Date Line lies between them; crossing that line going east, the date goes back one day.
fact: If everyone used London time, noon in Sydney would come in the middle of the night.
exercise: Why do different places need different clock times? | The Earth spins, so places face the Sun at different times | The Sun is much bigger over some countries than over others | Some countries are a lot closer to the Sun than others are | Clocks run faster in hot countries and slower in cold ones
exercise: How many degrees does the Earth turn in one hour? | 15 | 24 | 360 | 60
exercise: If a place is east of you, its clocks are usually... | Ahead of yours | Behind yours | The same as yours | Stopped at noon
exercise: It is 9 am in London in January. What time is it in Sydney? | 8 pm | 9 pm | 10 pm | 8 am
exercise: It is 2 pm in London. What time is it in New York? | 9 am | 7 pm | 2 pm | 10 am
exercise: Charlotte leaves London at 11:00 and flies 8 hours to New York. What time is it in New York when she lands? | 14:00 | 19:00 | 11:00 | 16:00
exercise: What time is it in London when Charlotte lands? | 19:00 | 14:00 | 03:00 | 16:00
exercise: Grandma calls from Kyiv at 7 pm Kyiv time. What time is it in London? | 5 pm | 9 pm | 7 pm | 4 pm
exercise: Dad rings Sophie at 6 pm in Manchester in winter. What time is it in Dubai? | 10 pm | 2 pm | 6 pm | 8 pm
exercise: A game event starts at 18:00 UTC. What time is that in Italy in summer? | 20:00 | 16:00 | 18:00 | 19:00
exercise: What is UTC? | The world's standard time, almost the same as Greenwich Mean Time | The time in Washington, used by all Americans and by the rest of the world | A clock that runs only on aeroplanes | The time in whichever country is biggest
exercise: What do pilots call UTC? | Zulu time | Pilot time | Sky time | Alpha time
exercise: A match kicks off at 20:00 in a country 7 hours behind Ollie. What time is it for Ollie? | 03:00 the next day | 13:00 the same day | 20:00 the same day | 01:00 the next day
exercise: Which very wide country uses just one time zone? | China | Russia | The USA | Australia
exercise: About how many main time zones are there? | About 24 | About 12 | About 7 | About 100
exercise: Ivan leaves New Zealand on Sunday night, flies east over the date line and lands in Hawaii on Sunday morning. Why? | Crossing the date line going east, the date goes back a day | The plane flew backwards in time | Hawaii uses a different calendar | His ticket had the wrong day printed
test: Jake's cousin in Sydney, in her pyjamas: "Mate, it's 8 at night here, yeah nah, can't play." ("Yeah nah" means no.) Why is it night there? | Sydney is far east of London, so its evening comes 11 hours earlier | Sydney is closer to the Moon than London | Australians always go to bed at 9 am | Sydney is 11 hours behind London, so it's still last night's evening there
test: Ellie: "Why don't we all use one time? Easier, innit." What would happen? | Noon would be dark in half the world | Everyone would wake at the same sunrise | The Sun would rise at the same moment everywhere | Nothing at all would change for anyone
test: Charlotte: "We leave at 11 and fly 8 hours, so we land at 7 pm, simples." What's wrong? | 7 pm is London time; in New York it's 2 pm | Nothing, it will be 7 pm in New York | She'll land at 3 am in New York | She'll land at 11 am in New York
test: Ivan: "I landed before I took off, I'm a time traveller, no cap!" ("No cap" means honestly.) What really happened? | He crossed the date line going east, and the date went back a day | The plane flew faster than time itself, so it arrived before leaving | He flew for minus fourteen hours, which planes can do over the ocean | His watch broke over the ocean and started running backwards in time
test: Amir's dad, at 2 pm in London: "The New York lot are just starting their day." Why? | New York is 5 hours behind, so it's 9 am there | New Yorkers start work at 2 pm | New York is 5 hours ahead, so it's 7 pm | Offices everywhere open at the same moment
test: Zara: "Why's Babusia (grandma) always ringing so early for dinner?" She rings at 7 pm Kyiv time. What time is it in London? | 5 pm | 9 pm | 7 pm | 3 pm
test: Sophie's dad, in Manchester in winter: "I'll bell you at 6." ("Bell" means phone.) He rings at 6 his time. Why is Sophie, in Dubai, cross? | It's 10 pm in Dubai and she was asleep | It's 2 pm in Dubai and she's at the pool | It's 6 am in Dubai and she's still in bed | It's 6 pm in Dubai, so nothing is wrong
test: Luca, in Rome, Italy, in July, sees "Event at 18:00 UTC" and sets his alarm for 18:00. What happens? | He's 2 hours early; it starts at 20:00 his time | He's exactly on time | He's 2 hours late | He misses it by a whole day
test: Max: "Pilots must get proper muddled flying through loads of zones." What do they do? | They use UTC, one world time, so 14:00 is the same moment everywhere | They change their watches every fifteen minutes, all the way across the sky | They use the time of whatever country is below | They use no clocks at all, only the Sun
test: What would happen to the width of a one-hour time zone if the Earth spun once every 12 hours? | It would be about 30 degrees wide | It would stay 15 degrees wide, as now | It would shrink to about 7 degrees | Time zones would disappear altogether
test: Noah, a Yorkshire lad: "Earth turns 360 degrees in 24 hours, so how wide's a time zone, like?" | About 15 degrees | About 24 degrees | About 36 degrees | About 1 degree
test: A World Cup final kicks off at 20:00 local time in a country 7 hours behind you. Your mate: "Easy, that's 1 pm for us." What's right? | It's 3 am the next day for you | Your mate's right, 1 pm | It's 8 pm for you as well | It's 7 pm for you
exam: Given that the Earth completes one rotation of 360 degrees in 24 hours, determine the angle through which it rotates in one hour. | Fifteen degrees | Twenty-four degrees | Thirty-six degrees | Sixty degrees
exam: A flight departs London at 11:00 London time and is of eight hours' duration; New York is five hours behind London. Ascertain the local time of arrival in New York. | 14:00 | 19:00 | 16:00 | 03:00
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning places situated to the east of an observer. | Their clocks are, in general, ahead of those of the observer | Their clocks are, in general, behind those of the observer | Their clocks invariably coincide with those of the observer | Their clocks are regulated by the phases of the Moon
exam: Identify the principal purpose of Coordinated Universal Time. | To provide one standard time that prevents confusion between zones | To replace all local clock times throughout the world with that of Washington | To regulate the hours of sunrise and sunset in every country of the world | To determine the dates of the principal religious festivals each year
